WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.255 --> 00:00:01.980
SARA ROBINSON: Human
vision is amazingly

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.660
beautiful and complex.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.100
It all started
billions of years ago

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.700
when small organisms
developed a mutation that

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.530
made them sensitive to light.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.570
Fast forward to today,
and there is an abundance

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.070
of life on the
planet which all have

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.379
very similar visual systems.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.570
They include eyes
for capturing light,

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.680
receptors in the brain
for accessing it,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.400
and a visual cortex
for processing it.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.200
Genetically engineered
and balanced pieces

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.210
of a system which
help us do things

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.970
as simple as
appreciating a sunrise.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.070
But this is really
just the beginning.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.500
In the past 30 years, we've
made even more strides

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.110
to extending this amazing visual
ability, not just to ourselves,

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.129
but to machines as well.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:41.670
The first type of
photographic camera

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.720
was invented around 1816 where a
small box held a piece of paper

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.610
coated with silver chloride.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.590
When the shutter was
open, the silver chloride

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.140
would darken where it
was exposed to light.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.680
Now, 200 years later, we have
much more advanced versions

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.275
of the system that can capture
photos right into digital form.

00:00:59.275 --> 00:01:01.650
So we've been able to closely
mimic how the human eye can

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.300
capture light and color.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.150
But it's turning out that
that was the easy part.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.360
Understanding what's in the
photo is much more difficult.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.130
Consider this picture.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.530
My human brain can look
at it and immediately know

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.862
that it's a flower.

00:01:14.862 --> 00:01:16.320
Our brains are
cheating since we've

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.530
got a couple million years
worth of evolutionary context

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.170
to help immediately
understand what this is.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.630
But a computer doesn't
have that same advantage.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.390
To an algorithm, the
image looks like this--

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.390
just a massive array of
integer values which represent

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.760
intensities across
the color spectrum.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.900
There's no context here,
just a massive pile of data.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.975
It turns out that the context is
the crux of getting algorithms

00:01:39.975 --> 00:01:42.540
to understand image
content in the same way

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.220
that the human brain does.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.440
And to make this work,
we use an algorithm

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.810
very similar to how the
human brain operates

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.730
using machine learning.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.950
Machine learning allows
us to effectively train

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.680
the context for a data set
so that an algorithm can

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.510
understand what
all those numbers

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.356
in a specific organization
actually represent.

00:02:00.356 --> 00:02:02.730
And what if we have images
that are difficult for a human

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:03.690
to classify?

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.940
Can machine learning
achieve better accuracy?

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.900
For example, let's take a look
at these images of sheep dogs

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.360
and mops where it's
pretty hard, even for us,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.090
to differentiate
between the two.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.590
With the machine
learning model, we

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.470
can take a bunch of images
of sheep dogs and mops,

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.459
and as long as we
feed it enough data,

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:23.250
it will eventually be
able to properly tell

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.080
the difference between the two.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.380
Computer vision is taking on
increasingly complex challenges

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.480
and is seeing
accuracy that rivals

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.180
humans performing the same
image recognition tasks.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.820
But like humans, these
models aren't perfect.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.440
They do sometimes make mistakes.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.020
The specific type of neural
network that accomplishes this

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.620
is called a convolutional
neural network or CNN.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.750
CNNs work by breaking
an image down

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.780
into smaller groups of
pixels called a filter.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.926
Each filter is a
matrix of pixels,

00:02:51.926 --> 00:02:53.800
and the network does a
series of calculations

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.170
on these pixels comparing
them against pixels

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.140
in a specific pattern the
network is looking for.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.850
In the first layer
of a CNN, it is

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.400
able to detect high-level
patterns like rough edges

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.630
and curves.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.640
As the network performs
more convolutions,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.710
it can begin to identify
specific objects

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.280
like faces and animals.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.200
How does a CNN know
what to look for

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.090
and if its prediction
is accurate?

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.700
This is done through a large
amount of labeled training

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.750
data.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.770
When the CNN starts, all of the
filter values are randomized.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.160
As a result, its initial
predictions make little sense.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.700
Each time the CNN
makes a prediction

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.420
against labeled data, it
uses an error function

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.250
to compare how close
its prediction was

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.290
to the image's actual label.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.710
Based on this error
or loss function,

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.340
the CNN updates
its filter values

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.020
and starts the process again.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:44.310
Ideally, each iteration performs
with slightly more accuracy.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.260
What if instead of
analyzing a single image,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.780
we want to analyze a video
using machine learning?

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.350
At its core, a video is just
a series of image frames.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.640
To analyze a video we can build
on our CNN for image analysis.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.270
In still images, we can use
CNNs to identify features.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.770
But when we move
to video, things

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.260
get more difficult since
the items we're identifying

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.910
might change over time.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.480
Or, more likely, there's context
between the video frames that's

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.400
highly important to labeling.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.020
For example, if
there's a picture

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.940
of a half full
cardboard box, we might

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.899
want to label it packing a box
or unpacking a box depending

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:21.660
on the frames
before and after it.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.700
This is where CNNs
come up lacking.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.370
They can only take into
account spatial features,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.880
the visual data in an image, but
can't handle temporal or time

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.420
features-- how a frame is
related to the one before it.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.870
To address this issue, we have
to take the output of our CNN

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.790
and feed it into
another model which

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.310
can handle the temporal
nature of our videos.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.070
This type of model is called
a recurrent neural network

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.885
or RNN.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:48.890
While a CNN treats groups
of pixels independently,

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:50.930
an RNN can retain
information about what

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.020
it's already processed and use
that in its decision making.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.340
RNNs can handle many types
of input and output data.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.350
In this example of
classifying videos,

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.750
we train the RNN by passing
it a sequence of frame

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:06.590
descriptions-- empty box,
open box, closing box--

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:07.820
and finally, a label--

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.180
packing.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.480
As the RNN processes
each sequence,

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.870
it uses a loss or error function
to compare its predicted output

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.580
with the correct label.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.430
Then it adjusts the weights and
processes the sequence again

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.734
until it achieves
a higher accuracy.

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:23.900
The challenge of these
approaches to image and video

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.940
models, however,
is that the amount

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.310
of data we need to
truly mimic human vision

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.050
is incredibly large.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.480
If we train our
model to recognize

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:35.150
this picture of a duck, as long
as we're given this one picture

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.860
with this lighting,
color, angle, and shape,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.200
we can see that it's a duck.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.320
But if you change any of that
or even just rotate the duck,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.170
the algorithm might not
understand what it is anymore.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.750
Now, this is the
big picture problem.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.150
To get an algorithm to truly
understand and recognize

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.547
image content the way
the human brain does,

00:05:52.547 --> 00:05:54.380
you need to feed it
incredibly large amounts

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.800
of data of millions of objects
across thousands of angles

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.159
all annotated and
properly defined.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.700
The problem is so
big, that if you're

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.250
a small startup or a
company lean on funding,

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.010
there's just no resources
available for you to do that.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.610
This is why technologies like
Google Cloud Vision and Video

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:11.900
can help.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:14.930
Google digests and filters
millions of images and videos

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.270
to train these APIs.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.700
We've trained a network to
extract all kinds of data

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.160
from images and video so
that your application doesn't

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.180
have to.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.190
With just one REST
API request, we're

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.740
able to access a powerful
pre-trained model that

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.090
gives us all sorts of data.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.010
Here's how easy it is to call
the Cloud Vision API with cURL.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:37.070
I'll send this image
to the API, and here's

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.500
the response we get back.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.470
Billions of years since the
evolution of our sense of sight

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:44.150
we found that computers
are on their way

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:47.332
to matching human vision, and
it's all available as an API.

00:06:47.332 --> 00:06:49.790
If you'd like to know more
about the Cloud Vision and Video

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.569
APIs, check out their product
pages at the links here

00:06:52.569 --> 00:06:54.110
to see how you can
easily add machine

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:55.590
learning to your application.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:56.423
Thanks for watching.

00:06:56.423 --> 00:06:59.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

